2 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]}]]}]]}]
37:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-16","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-8","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What is Sadhana?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Sadhana (Sanskrit: 'accomplishment' or 'practice') refers to any daily spiritual discipline undertaken with regularity and intention. It is the core of a practitioner's personal spiritual life — the practices done not for an audience or occasion, but as a committed daily offering."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"How long should a daily spiritual practice be?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Even 20-30 minutes daily is transformative. The tradition suggests at minimum one hour — 30 minutes of Japa and 30 minutes of meditation. Consistency over duration: 20 minutes every day beats 3 hours once a week."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What is Brahma Muhurta and why is it important?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Brahma Muhurta is the period 96 minutes before sunrise. The mind is naturally quiet before the day's activity begins, and Sattvic (pure) energy is at its peak. Practices done in this window are considered 4x more effective than those done at other times."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What practices make up a complete daily Sadhana?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"A complete Sadhana typically includes: purification (bath, clean clothes), pranayama (breathwork), Japa (mantra repetition), meditation, study of scripture (Svadhyaya), and surrender (offering the day to the Divine). Most practitioners include 2–3 of these."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"Should I practice before or after eating?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Always before. A full stomach draws blood to the digestive system and makes meditation dull and sleepy. The ancient rule: Sadhana on an empty stomach, or at minimum 2 hours after eating."}]]}],["$","div","5",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What if I miss a day of Sadhana?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"The tradition is clear: do not be disturbed by a missed day. Simply return the next day. The Sanskrit term is Abhyasa — repeated, sustained effort over time. One missed day within years of practice is negligible."}]]}],["$","div","6",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"Can I practice spiritually without a guru?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Yes, though a qualified teacher accelerates progress significantly. Begin with foundational texts (Bhagavad Gita, Patanjali's Yoga Sutras), choose one practice (Japa is most accessible), and practice consistently. The tradition says a sincere seeker attracts the right guidance."}]]}],"$L3d"]}]]}]
38:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gradient-to-br from-orange-950/30 to-background rounded-3xl border border-orange-900/30 p-10 text-center","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-4","children":"Ready to find your path?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto","children":"Take the Faith Finder — a 5-minute quiz that maps your temperament to the specific tradition, practice, and philosophy within Sanatan Dharma that fits you."}],["$","$La",null,{"href":"/faith-finder","children":["$","button",null,{"className":"inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap font-medium ring-offset-background focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50 [&_svg]:pointer-events-none [&_svg]:size-4 [&_svg]:shrink-0 bg-orange-500 text-white hover:bg-orange-600 h-14 px-8 rounded-full text-lg shadow-lg shadow-orange-900/20 transition-transform hover:scale-105","children":["Start the Faith Finder",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right ml-2 w-5 h-5","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]}]]}]
